page('Problem 14. Regularization and small singular values')
h('1. Expand the pseudoinverse solution')
eq(r'x=A^+b=\sum_{i=1}^{r}\frac{u_i^Tb}{\sigma_i}v_i.')
p('This follows by multiplying V_r Sigma_r-inverse U_r-transpose by b. Only positive singular values are inverted; null-space coordinates are zero.')
h('2. Noise amplification')
eq(r'\Delta x=A^+\Delta b=\sum_{i=1}^{r}\frac{u_i^T\Delta b}{\sigma_i}v_i,\qquad \left\|\Delta x\right\|_2\le\frac{\left\|\Delta b\right\|_2}{\sigma_r}.')
p('A perturbation delta times u_r produces delta/sigma_r times v_r. If sigma_r is tiny, even a small noise component can dominate the recovered coefficient. Not every perturbation reaches the bound; amplification depends on its singular-direction projections.')
h('3-4. Truncated-SVD regularization')
eq(r'x_k=\sum_{i=1}^{k}\frac{u_i^Tb}{\sigma_i}v_i,\qquad \left\|\Delta x_k\right\|_2\le\frac{\left\|\Delta b\right\|_2}{\sigma_k}.')
p('Keep only sufficiently large singular values. Discarded directions contribute no noise amplification, and the retained worst-case amplification is reduced. The cost is bias: genuine solution components in discarded directions are lost. The bound applies for k >= 1; at k = 0 the estimate is zero.')
h('5. Tikhonov regularization')
p('For lambda > 0, minimizing the squared residual plus lambda times the squared solution norm gives a unique solution even when A is rank deficient:')
eq(r'x_\lambda=(A^TA+\lambda I)^{-1}A^Tb=\sum_{i=1}^{r}\frac{\sigma_i}{\sigma_i^2+\lambda}(u_i^Tb)v_i.')
p('Relative to the pseudoinverse coefficient, TSVD uses a hard filter equal to one for retained modes and zero otherwise. Tikhonov uses the smooth filter sigma_i squared divided by sigma_i squared plus lambda. Its direct amplification factor tends to zero for small sigma_i and is bounded by 1/(2 sqrt(lambda)), because sigma_i squared plus lambda is at least 2 sigma_i sqrt(lambda).')
h('6. Choosing lambda')
p('As lambda decreases toward zero, the estimate approaches the minimum-norm pseudoinverse solution and may fit noise. As lambda grows, the estimate shrinks and bias increases. Select lambda using the noise scale, an independent validation criterion, a discrepancy principle or an L-curve rather than an unexamined universal constant. In this assignment lambda multiplies the penalty directly; a convention using lambda squared changes the denominator accordingly.')

page('Problem 15. Why SVD is a fundamental tool')
p('Singular value decomposition is fundamental because it describes a linear map through orthonormal directions and nonnegative scales. For a rectangular matrix, ordinary eigenvectors may not even be defined, while SVD always exists. Its right factor chooses input coordinates, its diagonal factor stretches them, and its left factor chooses output coordinates. A sphere therefore becomes an ellipsoid, possibly collapsed into a lower-dimensional subspace. This geometric description links the entries of a matrix to its actual action on vectors.')
p('Singular values also provide a precise language for rank and sensitivity. Exact rank counts the positive singular values; numerical rank requires a tolerance tied to rounding and data uncertainty. The largest singular value is the spectral norm, and the squared Frobenius norm is the sum of their squares. For an invertible square matrix, the ratio between the largest and smallest singular values is its spectral condition number. A small final singular value identifies a direction in which the data barely change even when the input changes substantially. Attempting to invert such a direction can amplify noise.')
p('For least squares, SVD separates observable coefficients from unobservable ones. The positive modes determine the fitted output, while the null-space directions leave that output unchanged. The pseudoinverse inverts only positive modes and sets the remaining coordinates to zero, yielding the unique minimum-norm least-squares solution. This handles rectangular and rank-deficient systems in the same framework. It also exposes why directly solving normal equations can be unreliable: forming the Gram matrix squares the positive singular-value condition ratio.')
p('A further reason for the importance of SVD is that truncation is mathematically optimal. Keeping the first k singular terms gives a best approximation among all matrices of rank at most k in spectral and Frobenius norms. The error is determined exactly by the omitted singular values. This turns dimensionality reduction into a measurable tradeoff rather than an arbitrary removal of entries. When the spectrum decays quickly, a few modes preserve most of the global information. However, this guarantee concerns matrix norms, so it does not automatically preserve every visually or scientifically important feature.')
p('The same ideas connect numerical linear algebra with data science. For centered observations, the right singular vectors are PCA directions and their squared singular values determine explained variance. Image compression stores a low-rank factorization, although model precision and file encoding must be counted separately from scalar savings. In noisy inverse problems, truncated SVD and Tikhonov filters suppress directions that are too unstable to recover reliably. The generated data experiment in this report illustrates the distinction: retaining more noise modes improves the fit to the noisy matrix while worsening recovery of the clean signal.')
p('SVD therefore supplies one coherent framework for geometry, approximation, estimation and stability. It is not always the cheapest computation for a very large matrix, and data preprocessing or task-specific validation remains necessary. Its value is that it makes those decisions interpretable: one can identify the retained subspace, quantify the discarded tail, recognize a null space and assess the effect of noise before trusting a computed answer.')

page('Optional challenge. Complex singular value decomposition')
h('1. Hermitian positive semidefiniteness')
eq(r'(A^*A)^*=A^*A,\qquad z^*A^*Az=\left\|Az\right\|_2^2\ge0.')
p('The conjugate-transpose identity proves that the Gram matrix is Hermitian. Its quadratic form is real and nonnegative for every complex vector z, so it is positive semidefinite.')
h('2. Derive the complex SVD')
p('The Hermitian spectral theorem gives A-star A = V Lambda V-star with V unitary and nonnegative real eigenvalues. For each positive eigenvalue set sigma_i to its square root and u_i = Av_i/sigma_i. Then:')
eq(r'u_i^*u_j=\frac{v_i^*A^*Av_j}{\sigma_i\sigma_j}=\delta_{ij}.')
p('Complete these vectors to an orthonormal basis of complex m-dimensional space. The zero-eigenvalue right modes satisfy Av_i = 0. Hence AV = U Sigma and A = U Sigma V-star, where U and V are unitary and Sigma is real nonnegative rectangular diagonal.')
h('3. Singular values of A and its adjoint')
eq(r'A^*=V\Sigma^T U^*,\qquad AA^*=U\Sigma\Sigma^T U^*,\qquad A^*A=V\Sigma^T\Sigma V^*.')
p('Sigma is real, so its adjoint is its transpose. Both Gram matrices have the same r positive eigenvalues sigma_i squared; their zero eigenvalue multiplicities are m-r and n-r. The singular-value lists of A and A-star, each of length min(m,n), are identical including zeros. The Gram spectra can contain different numbers of additional zeros for rectangular matrices.')
h('4. Spectral norm')
eq(r'\left\|Ax\right\|_2^2=\sum_{i=1}^{r}\sigma_i^2\left|v_i^*x\right|^2\le\sigma_1^2\left\|x\right\|_2^2.')
p('Unitary factors preserve the complex Euclidean norm. Equality holds at x = v_1 when A is nonzero, proving that its spectral norm is sigma_1.')
h('5. Transpose versus conjugate transpose')
p('The transpose only interchanges rows and columns; the conjugate transpose additionally conjugates every entry. The complex inner product conjugates its first argument, so unitarity uses V-star V = I. Using V-transpose would generally destroy this norm-preserving identity. For a real matrix the conjugation changes nothing and V-star equals V-transpose.')

